[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
The issue is not a network reachability problem; it is an application/service failure on the Office B server (srv1). From PC1 we can ping srv1 successfully and sustain high throughput (iperf ~83 Mbit/s), proving L2/L3 path, routing, and bandwidth between Office A and B are healthy. However, TCP connection attempts from PC1 to common services on srv1 (22/80/139/445/2049) all fail. The same failures occur from a local Office B client (pc3), ruling out inter-site ACLs and localizing the problem to the server itself.

On srv1, systemd reports key services inactive: apache2 is “Active: inactive (dead)” and sshd is “Active: inactive (dead)”. netstat shows no listeners on 22/80/139/445; only DNS (port 53) is bound on 10.5.0.4. With no daemons listening, clients’ connection attempts appear to “time out”/fail even though the host is reachable, matching the user’s symptoms.

Root cause: application services on srv1 (at least Apache and SSH; SMB likely absent or stopped) were left stopped after last night’s change.

Corrective fix:
- On srv1: systemctl start apache2 ssh (and start/enable smbd if file shares are required).
- Ensure they remain enabled: systemctl enable apache2 ssh smbd.
- Verify: netstat -tuln shows listeners on 0.0.0.0:80, :22, and :445; test from PC1/pc3 again.
- If starts fail, review yesterday’s change/configs (ports, binds, SELinux/firewall rules). Currently, the network fabric requires no changes. 
=== END ===